              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "In Massachusetts, the maximum amount you can collect for unemployment depends on whether you have dependents and how many weeks of benefits you qualify for. \n\n### 1. Maximum Weekly Benefit Amount\n* **Standard Maximum:** **$1,105 per week** (effective for claims filed after October 5, 2025, and remaining in effect through October 2026). \n* **Dependency Allowance:** If you have dependents (children under 18, or full-time students under 24) and you are their main source of support, you can receive an additional **$25 per week per dependent**. \n  * This allowance is added *on top* of your weekly benefit (even if you are already at the $1,105 maximum), up to a cap of **50% of your weekly benefit amount**. For example, if you qualify for the maximum $1,105 and have two dependents, you could receive **$1,155 per week**.\n\n### 2. Maximum Duration (Number of Weeks)\n* **Standard Duration:** Massachusetts typically provides up to **26 weeks** of benefits.\n* **Extended Duration (Active):** Under state law, if the 12-month average unemployment rate in any of the state's metropolitan areas rises above 5.1%, the maximum duration automatically extends statewide to **30 weeks**. This 30-week extension was triggered and remains active, meaning you can currently collect for up to **30 weeks**.\n\n### 3. Maximum Total Amount You Can Collect\n* **At 30 Weeks (Current Active Cap):** If you qualify for the maximum weekly rate of $1,105 for the full 30 weeks, the total maximum standard benefit you can collect is **$33,150** (excluding dependency allowances).\n* **At 26 Weeks (Standard Cap):** If the state's unemployment rate triggers a drop back to the standard limit, the maximum total you can collect over 26 weeks is **$28,730**.\n\n***\n\n*Note: If you are looking into **Paid Family and Medical Leave (PFML)** instead of regular unemployment, the maximum weekly benefit for PFML is higher, capped at **$1,230.39 per week**.*",
